A||N||10||87ECO5||Breaking in a new engine||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||03-09-2001||11:02 AM||||Ran a search but didn't find this discussed anywhere.  If it has been, please point me to it - thanks.<P>Ed Parks just finished swapping out my 2.5 I4 for a remanufactured 3.4 V6 and I get to pick it up tomorrow.<P>Question - what is the proper way to break in a new motor?  I'm concerned because, to get the car home, I have to put 400+ highway miles on the new engine and I've heard this is a bad thing.<P>Any comments - either on general break-in rules or "running a new motor on the highway" - is greatly appreciated.<p>[This message has been edited by 87ECO5 (edited 03-09-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Alex4mula||03-09-2001||11:18 AM||||Your engine is almost alreay done. Hwy miles are not the best but that doesn't mean you screwed up the engine. I bought my 3.4 from Ed & he just told me to run it bellow 70MPH and bellow 4K RPM for 500 miles. After my 500 it went up to 6K RPM several times to cure myself  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ... just when the fun started! I did the same when I bougt my C5.<BR>Coming from an I4 you should feel a huge difference. Are you happy?? Bet you are  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||MooCow||03-09-2001||12:37 PM||||Yea Ed told me the same thing.  Only I had to travel about 800 miles to get it home.  I went about 70MPH the whole way but my RPM's were up around 3.5 cause I have a 4:10 gear ratio.  So far I have put a total of 900 on it since January.  I plan on taking it in the next week or so.  Also I heard that it is good to change the oil after 1,000 or so miles to help break the eingine in.  Is that true??  You are gonna love the 3.4.  Ed does excellent work.<P>                              Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny.  Soon will be Viper Yellow!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||03-09-2001||01:33 PM||||Yes. MooCow is right. At 500-1000 miles chage the oil too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||03-09-2001||03:33 PM||||The reason they don't recommend highway driving is because during the break in period the engine shouldn't run at the same speed for more than 1-2 minutes in a row.  If you're on the highway speed up a bit for a minute, then slow down a few MPH for a minute, then speed up again.  Traditionally the recommendation was to remain under 50MPH as well although that's changed in the last 20 years. <P>You're generally doing this anyways by design during suburban driving so it's not a problem. Keep in mind that long idle periods aren't so great for a new/newly rebuilt engine either.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||03-09-2001||05:18 PM||||Ya know... I've been building motors and been around others building motors for almost 30 years. I've heard about every combination of "break-in" procedures there is including the ones mentioned. Over all those years and all those motors, I've never seen a motor fail that couldn't be explained by either a faulty part or poor building practices. None that you could pin point a problem with break-in.<P>The only thing I've observed is ring seating differences. I seen motors that the rings have seated almost as soon as the motor is started and others that take about 100 miles. My thought is this... Don't use the cruise control on your way home. This will ensure that your RPMs vary throughout the trip (Unless your driving through North Dakota where there are no hills or curves) and change your oil your fist chance when you return. This will get rid of all the assembly lube and any contaminates that may have wound up in the crankcase. The motor will be tight for a few hundred miles so it's good to watch your temperature and oil pressure especially if you get into heavy, slow traffic. Check you oil consumption at EVERY gas stop. Once you stop using oil, the rings have seated. Other than that, congratulations!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||BENGAL4||03-09-2001||06:48 PM||||Wahhhhhhh. Mines not finished yet. Have fun and be careful. Stop by on your way home if you want to.<P>Bill||||reg||10
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||03-09-2001||06:52 PM||||HUH?!?!||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||03-09-2001||08:58 PM||||The main thing is just don't beat on it for at least 500, preferably more, miles. If you have to do a long trip, do as ed says, keep the speed down. before you actually leave on the trip drive around the local area for a few hours in some light stop and go. stop and look at whatever is around while you at it. This will help seat in everything plus it will let you get used to it a bit before you are too far away if there is a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||PJ||03-09-2001||09:01 PM||||The first motor I rebuilt I finished late on a Thursday. Drove it around town for a couple of hours after work on Friday. On Saturday and Sunday I beat the snot out of it at Daytona in an hour and a half endurance race. It was fine afterwards.<BR>Shortly after the second rebuild I had to make a one day road trip 200 miles away and back on the interstate. Weather turned unseasonably cold and I forgot to reinstall the thermostat in the cooling system. The car ran the whole trip at about 1/8 on the temp gage. It also was fine afterwards.<BR>If you use moly rings and had the cylinders honed properly seating is not a problem. Just change the oil in short order like others here have pointed out.<P>BTW I changed the oil on that first rebuild after qualifying at Daytona that Saturday. I took it to the SafetyClean guy at the track like you're supposed to. He took out his sniffer and waved it over the drain pan. Told me to go dump that stuff in with the bad gas NOT into the oil recycling tank.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 03-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||03-09-2001||09:12 PM||||You do not want to flog the engine, but don't baby it too much. You need to do some good pulling or accelerating to get the cylinder pressures up to help seat the rings- something like leaving the tranny in 4th and let it pull down to about 1500 rpm's and do a half throttle acceleration up to about 3000 and let off to build high intake vacuume. Do this a several times and you should be in pretty good shape. Besides, Ed probably has already had the car out and about to get everything seated, just enjoy the trip!||||reg||1
